---
description: Apply when reviewing code — a diff, branch, PR, or your own completed work before declaring it done
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Review the change in context: diff first, then enough callers/callees to judge it. Trace the main path once; state the change's intent in one sentence; check intent against actual behavior — requirement mismatch is the highest-yield defect class.
- Hunt in order of yield: dropped/reinterpreted requirements · unhandled failure paths at every external boundary (error/empty/nil/timeout/partial) · shared state, races, missing idempotency, transaction boundaries · off-by-one, first/last/empty/huge, unicode, timezone, precision · injection, authz on every path, secrets in logs, unsafe deserialization · leaks on error paths, missing timeouts, retry without backoff · callers not updated, contracts broken, migrations missing · tests that would still pass with the fix reverted.
- Verify every candidate before reporting: trace the actual failure path and construct the concrete trigger ("empty list → total stays None → line 88 TypeError"). No concrete scenario → discard; it's a vibe, not a finding. Label CONFIRMED (traced) or PLAUSIBLE (+ what would confirm it).
- Report `SEVERITY [CONFIRMED|PLAUSIBLE] path:line — defect — trigger — fix`, most severe first: BLOCKER (wrong results, data loss, security, crash) / MAJOR (real failure, rarer conditions) / MINOR (fragile, misleading). Cap ~10; style feedback one line max, only when it hides a bug.
- Zero findings on a nontrivial change = shallow pass: re-check the top three categories, then make the clean report state what was checked. Never a bare LGTM.
- Tests passing is evidence, not proof — note what they don't cover. Judge the approach taken; don't rewrite it your way.
